# ☸️ Day 1 – What is Kubernetes?

> **Learning Goal:** Understand Kubernetes, why it is needed, and how it works with containers.

---

## 🎯 What I Learned

* Kubernetes is an **open-source platform for managing containerized applications**.
* It helps with **deployment, scaling, networking, and application management**.
* Kubernetes can manage applications running across multiple machines.
* It works based on a **desired state**.
* `kubectl` is commonly used to communicate with a Kubernetes cluster.

---

## 🤔 Why Do We Need Kubernetes?

Running a few containers manually is simple.

Managing many containers manually can become difficult.

### Without Kubernetes

```text
Developer
    │
    ├── Container 1
    ├── Container 2
    ├── Container 3
    └── Container 4
```

### Problems

| Problem           | Example                                      |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Container failure | What if a container crashes?                 |
| Scaling           | What if traffic suddenly increases?          |
| Multiple machines | How should workloads be distributed?         |
| Updates           | How can an application be updated safely?    |
| Management        | How can many containers be managed together? |

### With Kubernetes

```text
Developer
    │
    ▼
Kubernetes
    │
    ├── Pod
    ├── Pod
    ├── Pod
    └── Pod
```

Kubernetes provides mechanisms for scaling, failover, service discovery, rollouts, and other workload-management tasks.

---

## 🧠 Kubernetes in Simple Words

I think of Kubernetes as a **manager for containerized applications**.

```text
Docker
   ↓
Build & Run Containers

Kubernetes
   ↓
Manage Containerized Workloads
```

### Simple Comparison

| Docker                          | Kubernetes                       |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Builds container images         | Manages containerized workloads  |
| Runs containers                 | Manages Pods and workloads       |
| Works mainly at container level | Works at cluster level           |
| Packages applications           | Deploys and manages applications |

> **Easy way to remember:**
> **Docker → Container**
> **Kubernetes → Manage Containers**

---

## 🏗️ Basic Kubernetes Structure

```mermaid
graph TD
    User["Developer"] --> K["Kubernetes Cluster"]
    K --> CP["Control Plane"]
    K --> N1["Worker Node"]
    K --> N2["Worker Node"]
    N1 --> P1["Pod"]
    N2 --> P2["Pod"]
```

A Kubernetes cluster consists of a **control plane** and worker machines called **nodes**. The nodes run application Pods, while the control plane manages the cluster.

---

## 🧩 Important Kubernetes Terms

| Term              | Simple Meaning                                     |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| **Cluster**       | Complete Kubernetes environment                    |
| **Control Plane** | Manages the cluster                                |
| **Node**          | Machine that runs workloads                        |
| **Pod**           | Smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes             |
| **Container**     | Runs the application                               |
| **kubectl**       | Command-line tool used to interact with Kubernetes |

---

## 🔄 Basic Kubernetes Workflow

```text
Developer
    │
    ▼
  kubectl
    │
    ▼
API Server
    │
    ▼
Kubernetes Cluster
    │
    ▼
   Node
    │
    ▼
   Pod
    │
    ▼
Container
```

The Kubernetes API is the main interface through which users and Kubernetes components communicate with the cluster. `kubectl` uses this API to perform operations.

